A1. What husbands do when their wives play bridge. ~ DISHES
A6. To make a touching scene. ~ BORROW
A12. This has been hailed from many corners. ~ TAXI
A13. The answer to a maiden's prayer. ~ ADORER
A14. Father, dear father. ~ PA
A16. Tnis was picked for one of the best smellers. ~ LILAC
A19. The Stork Co. put in a large bill to bring 10 pounds of this heir-male. ~ BOY
A20. A high light in Egyptian history. ~ RA
A21. Most farmers do not like to grow this. ~ OLD
A23. Caesar had nothing up his sleeve when he put this over. ~ TOGA
A24. Big-time stuff. (plur.) ~ ERAS
A26. You could make a liar out of this by changing its viewpoint. ~ RAIL
A28. A trifle she picked up in Paris. ~ SOU
A29. A nom de plume. ~ SLOE
A31. Out on a limb. ~ TREED
A33. One feels pretty good when full of this. ~ GLEE
A36. The seat of the pane. ~ SASH
A38. These should know the ropes. ~ TARS
A40. Mumps and measles at the same time, ~ ILLS
A42. The day the ghost walks. ~ PAY
A43. A type that you find in seminaries and harems. ~ EM
A45. Fists that are not so good in a fight. ~ PACIFISTS
A48. The world gets this in the morning. ~ UP
A49. Pile of rugs. ~ NAP
A51. Webster says this is earth or clay containing carbonate of lime. ~ MARL
A52. Soup to nuts. ~ EATS
A54. A good thing to take on a train. (Railroad, not brides.) ~ TRIP
A56. Advertising gas. ~ NEON
A58. This will cause inflation and you will have more dough. ~ YEAST
A60. If you are honest you will do this to your word. ~ KEEP
A62. A humdinger. ~ WOW
A64. This comes after the wash every evening. (ref. Hor. No. 1.) ~ WIPE
A66. Classify. ~ SORT
A68. Here is where the English have had their lesson. ~ ETON
A70. Just another racket. ~ DIN
A71. Circular letters. ~ OO
A73. A circus nut. ~ PEA
A74. A request from old bill. ~ REMIT
A76. This Greek character is half nuts. ~ NU
A77. "At your service, Milord." ~ BUTLER
A79. Time for aspirin. ~ ACHE
A81. Where occidents seldom happen. ~ ORIENT
A82. You can get this in quite a small compass. ~ NEEDLE

D1. The low-down on Junior's report card. ~ DEPORTMENT
D2. What many people lived on during the depression. (abbr.) ~ ST
D3. Three-fourths of half. ~ HAL
D4. The only free thing at the circus. ~ EXIT
D5. Where fodder makes alcohol. (plur.) ~ SILOS
D6. An expert in the bawl game, and a home runner. ~ BABE
D7. Use common scents for this. (plur.) ~ ODORS
D8. Kings, queens and flushes. ~ ROYALS
D9. Do not stall on this. (abbr.) ~ RR
D10. Get it over quickly. ~ OER
D11. Some poor thing was skinned in order to make this. ~ WRAP
D15. Possessing or pertaining to wings. ~ ALAR
D17. All a dither. ~ AGOG
D18. Earmark of a fighter. ~ CAULIFLOWER
D22. When you go on this you may fall off. ~ DIET
D25. A bar made popular for ladies. ~ SOAP
D27. Take this to prevent that run-down feeling. ~ LEAP
D30. Dial Gen. 25-25. ~ ESAU
D32. A small drink. (Scotch) ~ DRAM
D34. Yale. ~ ELI
D35. Otherwise. ~ ELSE
D37. This will give you a line on one of those triangle affairs. ~ HYPOTENUSE
D39. To give the once over. ~ SCAN
D41. Stick around for a while. ~ STAY
D44. This might make some change in Germany. ~ MARK
D46. If this starts Ireland, it will be the finish of the British Empire. ~ IRE
D47. Irish hash. ~ STEW
D50. Father made the dough and Mother made these. ~ PIES
D53. Before mentioned. ~ SAID
D55. Brown, Jones and Robinson and their folks. ~ PEOPLE
D57. This gives promise of more money. ~ NOTE
D59. This may cause a revolution. ~ SPIN
D61. What flyers do to doll up. ~ PREEN
